TI  - Dairy peptides in effective blood glucose management
T2  - AUSTRALIAN JOURNAL OF DAIRY TECHNOLOGY
CP  - 9th Dairy Science World Series Conference
AB  - The burden of chronic diseases (including diabetes) is increasing and so strategies to prevent such diseases from developing or delaying their progress are of high importance. Extensive knowledge exists about the co-ingestion of certain dietary proteins, protein hydrolysates and amino acids with carbohydrate to augment the insulin response and to enhance glucose disposal in type 2 diabetic subjects. There is evidence that these are involved in the release of several hormones and can act as insulin secretagogues. In these studies, rather high and frequent doses were used over a relatively short time period. However, preliminary data of a randomised, placebo-controlled trial suggests that a single lower dose of a protein hydrolysate given to type 2 diabetes mellitus patients may also be beneficial to reduce postprandial glucose levels and increase insulin secretion. In view of the reduced sensitivity of the (I-cell to glucose in type 2 diabetes patients, these findings are of particular interest as they demonstrate that the capacity to secrete insulin in response to peptide stimuli remains intact and, accordingly, can be enhanced. Dietary strategies that effectively reduce the postprandial rise in blood glucose levels following carbohydrate intake have been associated with a reduced risk of developing full diabetes and cardiovascular complications.
