TI  - Beneficial Effects of a Topical Foam Cream Product in a Study on Subjects with Type 1 and Type 2 Diabetic Foot Skin Xerosis
T2  - DIABETES STOFFWECHSEL UND HERZ
AB  - Aim: The purpose of this double-blind randomized vehicle-controlled in-home use and clinical study was to evaluate the effectiveness of a topically applied medical device foam cream on dry skin of diabetic feet.
   Methods: Measurements of skin moisture were performed using corneometry (hydration) and transepidermal water loss (barrier function). General appearance (digital photography) of the skin was also evaluated. Subjective and objective evaluations of xerosis parameters were performed before, during and after the test period.
   Results: A significant increase in skin hydration of the feet treated with the active foam cream was observed compared to vehicle foam cream. Measurement of skin capacitance demonstrated a significant improvement when treated with the active foam cream.Transepidermal water loss (TEWL) was reduced for both treatments. After an application period of 2 weeks improved barrier function was observed for the active foam cream. Both, subject and clinical observations showed an improvement of visual skin appearance with the observed improvement being advantageous toward the active foam cream.
   Conclusion: The active medical device foam cream performed better regarding barrier function, hydration and overall visual skin appearance of the feet. All findings were well correlated and with the observed improvement favouring the active foam cream.
